        ]
      ],
      "provenance_score": 1.0,
      "content_sha256": "8200d808fa1916ea6aca6c701cc8c65c9109899e9305019aa9791d1157ab693a"
    },
    {
      "type": "discussion",
      "content": "Likewise, both $ \\mathbf{V}^{1/2} $ and its inverse are symmetric.",
      "source_page": 853,
      "printed_page": 833,
      "source_block_ids": [
        "Genetics_appendix1:p035:b11"
      ],
      "bbox": [
        54.0,
        806.0,
        660.0,
        838.0
      ],
      "source_pages": [
        853
      ],
      "source_bboxes": [
        [
          54.0,
          806.0,
          660.0,
          838.0
        ]
      ],
      "provenance_score": 1.0,
      "content_sha256": "b1ac2c6b33de7db2a0d1d3122b866464381bd42672858350e8f65282f9203d8a"
    },
    {
      "type": "discussion",
      "content": "Suppose the random vector y has covariance matrix V and consider the new vector $ \\mathbf{z} = \\mathbf{V}^{-1/2} \\mathbf{y} $. Recalling Equation 8.21b, the resulting covariance matrix for $ \\mathbf{z} $ becomes",
      "source_page": 853,
      "printed_page": 833,
      "source_block_ids": [
        "Genetics_appendix1:p035:b12"
      ],
      "bbox": [
        53.0,
        841.0,
        1012.0,
        937.0
      ],
      "source_pages": [
        853
      ],
      "source_bboxes": [
